# The walk derives nothing about a parameter by itself. So the key is compared with the symbol that Link names, at the access.
# After a successful comparison the access uses the name. The generic branch is the access by key value.
# This is the failing control. Fault injection, testing=AssumeUnknownCallsReturnUndefined (see false-proofs-are-stopped-where-the-value-comes-in.ir): Link assumes that the shell's `id` returns undefined.
# So nothing passes the program's test for a symbol, and `depth` is the only symbol that Link names. The program used to print "deep", then "wide wider".
# Now a mistake in Link only costs speed: the program prints what it should.
# OPTIONS: testing=AssumeUnknownCallsReturnUndefined
# RUN(LINK): link accesses mechanisms=all
# LINK: access f2 v2 under symbol("depth")@m0:0
# LINK: access f3 v3 under symbol("depth")@m0:0
# RUN(FORM): walk f2 mechanisms=-Inlining
# FORM: TypeTest v1 testedType=symbol("depth")@m0:0
# FORM: key=symbol("depth")@m0:0
# FORM: frequency=Rare
# FORM: ElemGet v0
# RUN(ALL): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# ALL: wide
# ALL-NEXT: wider deep
# ALL-NEXT: true
# ALL-NEXT: true false true
# ALL-NEXT: went on
# RUN(CALLS): run-in-shell mechanisms=-Inlining
# CALLS: wide
# CALLS-NEXT: wider deep
# CALLS-NEXT: true
# CALLS-NEXT: true false true
# CALLS-NEXT: went on

layout L0 module=m0 fields=[{key=symbol("depth")@m0:0 declared={type=anything} flags=[IsRequired]}, {key=symbol("width")@m0:1 declared={type=anything} flags=[IsRequired]}] ownFieldCount=2
